Three ways to set the port in `application.properties` or `application.yml`.

```properties
# application.properties
server.port=8081
```

```yaml
# application.yml
server:
  port: 8081
```

```properties
# Relaxed binding from env var — no code needed
# Env: SERVER_PORT=8081
# Spring maps SERVER_PORT → server.port automatically
```

**Command-line override** (wins over everything in files):

```bash
java -jar app.jar --server.port=8082
```

**Random port for integration tests** — the single most useful trick interviewers love:

```java
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
class ApiIntegrationTest {

    @LocalServerPort
    private int port;

    @Autowired
    private TestRestTemplate rest;

    @Test
    void healthEndpointResponds() {
        String url = "http://localhost:" + port + "/actuator/health";
        ResponseEntity<String> response = rest.getForEntity(url, String.class);
        assertThat(response.getStatusCode()).isEqualTo(HttpStatus.OK);
    }
}
```

`server.port=0` tells Spring Boot to pick any free port; `@LocalServerPort` injects the actual chosen port. This eliminates port conflicts in parallel CI test runs — a common source of flaky integration tests.

**Production pattern**: Never hardcode the port in packaged `application.properties` for production. Use `${PORT:8080}` so the same artifact respects a platform-supplied `PORT` env var (Heroku, Cloud Run, Cloud Foundry all set `PORT`):

```properties
server.port=${PORT:8080}
```

**Secrets — never commit passwords**. Use env-var substitution with a default for local dev only:

```properties
spring.datasource.password=${DB_PASSWORD:dev_only_password}
```

At deploy time, the platform injects `DB_PASSWORD` from its secrets manager:

- **Kubernetes**: `env: [{ name: DB_PASSWORD, valueFrom: { secretKeyRef: ... } }]`
- **AWS ECS**: task definition `secrets` section pulling from Secrets Manager or Parameter Store
- **HashiCorp Vault**: Spring Cloud Vault fetches secrets before startup
- **Cloud Foundry**: bound service provides credentials via `VCAP_SERVICES`

**Multiple DataSources** — when you have a primary and a secondary:

```properties
app.datasource.primary.url=jdbc:postgresql://primary:5432/db1
app.datasource.primary.username=primary_user
app.datasource.primary.password=${PRIMARY_DB_PASSWORD}

app.datasource.secondary.url=jdbc:mysql://secondary:3306/db2
app.datasource.secondary.username=secondary_user
app.datasource.secondary.password=${SECONDARY_DB_PASSWORD}
```

```java
@Configuration
public class DataSourceConfig {

    @Bean
    @Primary
    @ConfigurationProperties("app.datasource.primary")
    public DataSourceProperties primaryProps() {
        return new DataSourceProperties();
    }

    @Bean
    @Primary
    public DataSource primaryDataSource(@Qualifier("primaryProps") DataSourceProperties p) {
        return p.initializeDataSourceBuilder().type(HikariDataSource.class).build();
    }

    @Bean
    @ConfigurationProperties("app.datasource.secondary")
    public DataSourceProperties secondaryProps() {
        return new DataSourceProperties();
    }

    @Bean
    public DataSource secondaryDataSource(@Qualifier("secondaryProps") DataSourceProperties p) {
        return p.initializeDataSourceBuilder().type(HikariDataSource.class).build();
    }
}
```

The `@Primary` annotation tells Spring which datasource to inject when no qualifier is specified.

```properties
# Development (application-dev.properties)
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=create-drop
# Schema is recreated on each run; simple, predictable
# Optionally seed with src/main/resources/import.sql

# CI / Integration tests (application-test.properties)
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=create-drop
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:h2:mem:testdb
# Or: use Testcontainers with a real Postgres and ddl-auto=none + Flyway

# Staging (application-staging.properties)
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=validate
# Flyway runs migrations; Hibernate validates the result
spring.flyway.enabled=true
spring.flyway.locations=classpath:db/migration

# Production (application-prod.properties)
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=validate
spring.flyway.enabled=true
# Or:
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=none   # if you don't want Hibernate touching schema at all
```

**Example Flyway migration** — `V3__add_orders_priority_column.sql`:

```sql
ALTER TABLE orders
ADD COLUMN priority INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0;

CREATE INDEX idx_orders_priority ON orders(priority) WHERE priority > 0;
```

Flyway applies this once, records the version, and never applies it again. The next deploy sees V3 is applied and skips it. Concurrent deploys see the Flyway lock and wait.

**Testcontainers pattern for CI** — closer to production than H2:

```java
@SpringBootTest
@Testcontainers
class OrdersIntegrationTest {
    @Container
    static PostgreSQLContainer<?> postgres = new PostgreSQLContainer<>("postgres:16");

    @DynamicPropertySource
    static void props(DynamicPropertyRegistry reg) {
        reg.add("spring.datasource.url", postgres::getJdbcUrl);
        reg.add("spring.datasource.username", postgres::getUsername);
        reg.add("spring.datasource.password", postgres::getPassword);
    }
    // Flyway runs migrations; ddl-auto=validate verifies
}
```

Virtual threads change the picture entirely. With one property, Spring Boot runs **every request** on a new virtual thread rather than borrowing from a pool:

```properties
spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true
```

Requirements:
- **Java 21+** (virtual threads are a 21 GA feature)
- **Spring Boot 3.2+** (integration)
- **Tomcat 10.1+ or Jetty 12+** (embedded servlet containers that support virtual threads)

**What changes internally**:

```java
// Before (traditional):
// Tomcat picks worker from pool, runs Servlet.service() on it

// After (virtual threads):
// Tomcat creates a new virtual thread per request: Thread.ofVirtual().start(...)
```

**Implications**:

- **Thread pool sizing becomes mostly irrelevant** for I/O-bound handlers. Virtual threads are cheap — tens of thousands concurrently is fine.
- **Blocking I/O is OK again** — JDBC, `RestTemplate`, `HttpClient` all work efficiently. You don't need WebFlux or reactive programming to handle high concurrency.
- **`synchronized` blocks are a pitfall** — they **pin** virtual threads to carrier threads, losing the benefit. Prefer `ReentrantLock`.
- **ThreadLocal** works but has memory implications at scale — many virtual threads with ThreadLocals can retain significant memory.
- **Native code** (JNI) also pins threads — still usually fine, but watch for hot paths.

**Verifying virtual threads are active**:

```java
@GetMapping("/check")
public String check() {
    return Thread.currentThread().toString();
    // Platform: "Thread[http-nio-8080-exec-5,5,main]"
    // Virtual:  "VirtualThread[#42]/runnable@ForkJoinPool-1-worker-3"
}
```

**Migration path**: enable virtual threads, load-test, look for pinning (monitor with `-Djdk.tracePinnedThreads=short`), replace `synchronized` with `ReentrantLock` in pinned spots. Most Spring Boot apps see throughput gains and memory reduction with no other code changes.

```properties
# Time Tomcat waits for a client to send the request after TCP connect
# (not the time to process the request — that's driven by thread availability)
server.tomcat.connection-timeout=20000         # 20 seconds

# Keep-alive idle timeout — time an idle keep-alive connection stays open
server.tomcat.keep-alive-timeout=60000         # 60 seconds

# Max requests per keep-alive connection
server.tomcat.max-keep-alive-requests=100

# Max total time for reading the request body (slow-HTTP attack mitigation)
# Tomcat 9+: configured via Connector attribute; Boot exposes via customizer
```

For finer control (e.g. slow-HTTP mitigation), use a `WebServerFactoryCustomizer`:

```java
@Bean
public WebServerFactoryCustomizer<TomcatServletWebServerFactory> tomcatCustomizer() {
    return factory -> factory.addConnectorCustomizers(connector -> {
        connector.setProperty("connectionTimeout", "20000");
        connector.setProperty("maxSwallowSize", "-1");
        // Slow-HTTP mitigation:
        connector.setProperty("readTimeout", "10000");
        connector.setProperty("connectionUploadTimeout", "30000");
        connector.setProperty("disableUploadTimeout", "false");
    });
}
```

**What each inbound timeout covers**:

- `connection-timeout` — after TCP handshake, how long to wait for the first request bytes.
- `keep-alive-timeout` — after a response is sent, how long to hold the connection open for the next request.
- `connectionUploadTimeout` + `disableUploadTimeout=false` — timeout applied specifically during request body upload (prevents slow POST attacks).

**RestTemplate** with Apache HttpClient (recommended over `SimpleClientHttpRequestFactory`):

```java
@Bean
public RestTemplate restTemplate() {
    PoolingHttpClientConnectionManager connManager = new PoolingHttpClientConnectionManager();
    connManager.setMaxTotal(50);
    connManager.setDefaultMaxPerRoute(20);

    RequestConfig requestConfig = RequestConfig.custom()
        .setConnectTimeout(Timeout.ofSeconds(3))            // TCP connect
        .setResponseTimeout(Timeout.ofSeconds(10))          // read response body
        .setConnectionRequestTimeout(Timeout.ofSeconds(2))  // wait for pool connection
        .build();

    CloseableHttpClient httpClient = HttpClients.custom()
        .setConnectionManager(connManager)
        .setDefaultRequestConfig(requestConfig)
        .build();

    HttpComponentsClientHttpRequestFactory factory =
        new HttpComponentsClientHttpRequestFactory(httpClient);

    return new RestTemplate(factory);
}
```

**WebClient** with Netty HttpClient:

```java
@Bean
public WebClient webClient() {
    HttpClient httpClient = HttpClient.create()
        .option(ChannelOption.CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MILLIS, 3000)
        .responseTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(10))
        .doOnConnected(conn -> conn
            .addHandlerLast(new ReadTimeoutHandler(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS))
            .addHandlerLast(new WriteTimeoutHandler(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS)));

    return WebClient.builder()
        .clientConnector(new ReactorClientHttpConnector(httpClient))
        .build();
}
```

**Feign** (declarative client):

```properties
spring.cloud.openfeign.client.config.default.connect-timeout=3000
spring.cloud.openfeign.client.config.default.read-timeout=10000

# Per-client override
spring.cloud.openfeign.client.config.inventory-service.read-timeout=5000
```

**Sensible defaults for most services**:
- **Connect timeout**: 3 seconds (TCP should establish fast; longer usually means misconfigured DNS or firewall)
- **Read timeout**: 10-30 seconds depending on downstream SLA
- **Connection request timeout**: 2 seconds (how long to wait for a pool connection)

Never leave any of these at their default — `RestTemplate` defaults to **no timeout** on every setting.
